{"id":18547485599452,"type":"song","name":"Only the Lonely","artist":"Roy Orbison","year":1960,"weight":18,"info":{"cover":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/cover/dfed8ac30c6efcfbce66c05eb0139add/250x250-000000-80-0-0.jpg","album":"The Essential Roy Orbison","yt":"UTR2IlAJEyQ"},"artist_ref":{"id":244694073052595,"name":"Roy Orbison","info":{"img":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/artist/a05bdb2eed3a93309c518e7c89e362d0/1000x1000-000000-80-0-0.jpg"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","grp":"chart","list_year":null,"rank":20,"year":1960,"note":null,"total":6841,"scaruffi":null},{"id":132782249584307,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1960","src":"singles","grp":"billboard_year_end","list_year":1960,"rank":20,"year":1960,"note":"","total":100,"scaruffi":null},{"id":58489523060370,"name":"UK nº1 · 1960s","src":"singles","grp":"uk_number_ones","list_year":1960,"rank":108,"year":1960,"note":"2 semanas","total":187,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"An Orbison-Melson collaboration that became his breakthrough hit, making him a star. It had roots in Melson's song \"Cry,\" reworked with a vocal hook from a discarded \"Teen Angel\" knockoff called \"Come Back to Me My Love.\" The song's structure is notably bizarre, featuring alternating lead and backing vocals out of phase, creating five-bar phrases, and a bass line that prolongs chords to give the impression of a six-beat bar, blending elements of a twelve-bar blues and an eight-bar doo-wop song in an unusual 24-bar sequence (5, 6, 8, 5). Initially turned down by Elvis and the Everly Brothers, it reached number two in the US and number one in the UK. \"But it's that wrongness, that stringeness of course, along with Orbison's magnificent voice, that made the record a hit, expressing perfectly the confusion and disorientation felt by the song's protagonist.\"","year":1960,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Mus